From ad60c126653d76f276157aec63ede54b516f8745 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:09:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] [Perf][Qwen4Exp] Score dense BF16 linears as W8A8 int8
 on sm_80

The shipped FP8 checkpoint quantizes only the routed experts: its
quantization_config lists every dense linear (attention qkv/o, GDN
projections, shared MLP) in modules_to_not_convert, so those layers
load bf16 weights and run the unquantized GEMM dispatch. sm_80 int8
tensor cores run at twice the bf16 rate on half the weight bytes, and
a W8A8 shootout on this box measures CUTLASS int8 at 1.29-1.68x over
the deployed dense GEMM at the production prefill chunk (M=2048),
per-token activation quant included, with int8 losing only below ~2M
weight elements or K<1024.

VLLM_QWEN4EXP_DENSE_W8A8=1 (default off) walks the model once and
swaps the quant method of eligible bf16 dense linears to a subclass of
UnquantizedLinearMethod: round-quantize the loaded weight to
per-output-channel int8 at process_weights_after_loading (chunked by
row blocks so fp32 intermediates never materialize the whole weight),
then score prefill-sized batches with dynamic per-token quant plus
cutlass_scaled_mm. The exact-type check leaves layers claimed by
another method (draft W8A8 head, embedding methods, low-latency
skinny GEMMs) alone.

Everything else keeps today's path: decode batches below M=512 (all
captured sizes here are <= 80, so replays are untouched), biased
layers, routers, conv1d, indexer, PLE, MoE experts (grouped int8
loses on this box), batch-invariant mode, other architectures, and
every GPU but sm_80.

The int8 cache lives next to the bf16 weight so the fallback stays
live; costs roughly one extra byte per dense weight element, about
0.2 GB per stage here, listed for boot verification.
